  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Tuesday)\nIssued at 1254 PM CST WED NOV 25 2026\n\nAn upper-level trough is digging through the Plains and will\nbring widespread rain tonight through early Thursday morning. At\nthe surface, steady low-level moistening through the afternoon\nmay produce pockets of heavier rainfall at times. Strong to\nsevere storms are not expected with this system. Most locations\ncan expect 0.5 to 1.5 inches of rain through early Thursday\nmorning. Rain will come to an end, leaving most of Thanksgiving\nDay dry; however, low chances for rain remain across northern\ncounties due to wrap-around moisture. Forecast guidance has\nstruggled with low temperatures tonight into Thursday morning.\nPrevious guidance showed low 30s in the northwest by sunrise,\nwhich is unlikely given current trends, so adjustments were made.\nHourly guidance appears much more reasonable than daily minimum\nand maximum guidance, especially considering the following night\nis expected to be cooler than the previous one.\n\nA cold front clearing the area will leave dry and chilly\nconditions for Thanksgiving. A low chance of showers persists\nacross northern areas due to wrap-around moisture, but most\nlocations will remain dry. Breezy northwesterly winds will hold\nhigh temperatures in the mid-40s to low 50s on Thursday, with\ntemperatures potentially peaking early in the day before dropping\nthrough the afternoon. Overnight lows will fall into the mid-20s\nto low 30s Friday and Saturday morning. Southerly flow quickly\nresumes on Saturday, warming temperatures several degrees.\nEnsemble guidance remains uncertain for Sunday: some models show\nanother trough and front bringing widespread rain, while others\nsuggest a slower arrival or lighter precipitation. \n\n16",
